<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Beuno</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 640</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Beuno</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint Beuno was a Welsh abbot and missionary of the seventh century, one of the most important saints of north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. Tradition holds that he founded a monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clynnog-fawr">Clynnog Fawr</a> on the Llŷn peninsula and that he restored his niece Saint Winefride to life. Clynnog became a major pilgrimage centre on the route to Bardsey Island.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-beuno.jpg" alt="Saint Beuno" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolfgang Sauber · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St.Beuno_-_Fassade.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bangor, traditionally founded by Saint Deiniol in the sixth century, became the seat of the diocese, while Clynnog Fawr, associated with <b>Saint Beuno</b>, Penmon and Holyhead were important churches.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-gwynedd">Kingdom of Gwynedd · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Kingdom of Powys. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clynnog-fawr">Clynnog Fawr</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.02,-4.37&amp;year=630" aria-label="Clynnog Fawr on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4452 338 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Saint Beuno"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4902 608h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4902 608h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=53.02,-4.37&amp;year=630">What was here in 630 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
